1
00:00:04,700 --> 00:00:06,342
It works on my machine.

2
00:00:06,342 --> 00:00:11,483
Every developer has said it, and every operations engineer has heard it.

3
00:00:11,483 --> 00:00:13,553
On your laptop, the app runs.

4
00:00:13,553 --> 00:00:17,051
On your colleague's laptop, a library is missing.

5
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:21,047
And on the server, something else is different.

6
00:00:21,047 --> 00:00:23,614
Same code, three different results.

7
00:00:24,167 --> 00:00:28,391
Docker fixes this by packaging the app together with everything it needs:

8
00:00:28,391 --> 00:00:31,632
the exact language version, the libraries, the settings.

9
00:00:31,632 --> 00:00:36,898
That package is called an image, and it runs the same way on every machine that has Docker.

10
00:00:37,433 --> 00:00:40,950
In this two part course you will not just read about Docker.

11
00:00:40,950 --> 00:00:43,705
You will run containers, break them on purpose,

12
00:00:43,705 --> 00:00:49,508
investigate like a real engineer, fix them, and finish with a complete three container application.

13
00:00:51,267 --> 00:00:55,725
Everything in this video comes from one free repository on GitHub.

14
00:00:55,725 --> 00:00:58,359
The tutorial folder is the guided path.

15
00:00:58,359 --> 00:01:01,196
This video follows it, chapter by chapter.

16
00:01:01,733 --> 00:01:07,073
The labs are short workbooks you do on your own, and the docs explain each concept in depth.

17
00:01:07,633 --> 00:01:13,493
There are ten troubleshooting scenarios with real failures, and nineteen challenges with hidden solutions.

18
00:01:14,033 --> 00:01:16,622
And the capstone puts everything together.

19
00:01:16,622 --> 00:01:17,546
One more thing:

20
00:01:17,546 --> 00:01:22,415
every command you see here was executed automatically on a fresh Docker engine,

21
00:01:22,415 --> 00:01:25,127
and the output on screen is the real output.

22
00:01:25,127 --> 00:01:26,668
Nothing is typed by hand.

23
00:01:28,433 --> 00:01:29,973
You need three things.

24
00:01:29,973 --> 00:01:30,463
Docker:

25
00:01:30,463 --> 00:01:37,390
Docker Desktop on Windows or Mac, and on Windows use the W S L two backend and its Ubuntu terminal.

26
00:01:37,390 --> 00:01:39,069
On Linux, Docker Engine.

27
00:01:39,069 --> 00:01:43,198
The installation lesson in docs walks you through each one.

28
00:01:43,733 --> 00:01:46,265
Then Git, to clone the repository.

29
00:01:46,265 --> 00:01:48,573
The link is in the description.

30
00:01:49,133 --> 00:01:49,937
That is all.

31
00:01:49,937 --> 00:01:52,146
No cloud account, nothing to pay.

32
00:01:52,146 --> 00:01:56,030
Every command in the course runs from the repository root,

33
00:01:56,030 --> 00:02:00,181
so if a command cannot find a file, check where you are first.

34
00:02:01,933 --> 00:02:04,657
Before the first command, the big picture.

35
00:02:04,657 --> 00:02:08,030
You type commands into the Docker command line tool.

36
00:02:08,567 --> 00:02:13,751
It sends each request to the Docker engine, a background service that does the real work:

37
00:02:13,751 --> 00:02:17,653
building images, running containers, creating networks and volumes.

38
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:21,513
Images come from a registry, usually Docker Hub.

39
00:02:21,513 --> 00:02:25,655
The engine pulls them down, and can push your own images up.

40
00:02:26,200 --> 00:02:29,123
And from an image, the engine creates containers:

41
00:02:29,123 --> 00:02:33,120
running processes, isolated from each other and from your computer.

42
00:02:34,867 --> 00:02:38,938
If you remember one idea from this video, make it this one.

43
00:02:38,938 --> 00:02:41,146
An image is a read-only package:

44
00:02:41,146 --> 00:02:43,975
files, plus instructions for what to run.

45
00:02:43,975 --> 00:02:45,631
Think of it as a recipe.

46
00:02:46,167 --> 00:02:49,234
A container is what you get when you run an image.

47
00:02:49,234 --> 00:02:55,125
Like meals cooked from one recipe, you can create as many containers from one image as you like.

48
00:02:55,125 --> 00:02:56,781
Some running, some stopped.

49
00:02:57,333 --> 00:03:00,482
And deleting a container never deletes the image.

50
00:03:00,482 --> 00:03:02,153
You can always cook again.

51
00:03:03,900 --> 00:03:04,789
Let's start.

52
00:03:04,789 --> 00:03:10,120
Open a terminal in the repository folder and ask Docker for its version.

53
00:03:10,120 --> 00:03:10,786
One line:

54
00:03:10,786 --> 00:03:13,674
the version of the Docker command line.

55
00:03:13,674 --> 00:03:15,600
This video uses Docker 29.

56
00:03:16,133 --> 00:03:17,933
Compose is included too.

57
00:03:17,933 --> 00:03:20,033
We will need it in part two.

58
00:03:20,567 --> 00:03:23,001
Now run your very first container:

59
00:03:23,001 --> 00:03:24,648
docker run hello world.

60
00:03:24,648 --> 00:03:26,008
Look at the output.

61
00:03:26,008 --> 00:03:30,662
Unable to find image locally, so Docker pulls it from Docker Hub.

62
00:03:30,662 --> 00:03:31,020
Then:

63
00:03:31,020 --> 00:03:32,309
Hello from Docker.

64
00:03:32,309 --> 00:03:36,748
That line was printed by a program running inside a container.

65
00:03:36,748 --> 00:03:38,466
Your installation works.

66
00:03:40,200 --> 00:03:42,485
hello world exits immediately.

67
00:03:42,485 --> 00:03:45,456
Let's run something that keeps running:

68
00:03:45,456 --> 00:03:47,589
nginx, a popular web server.

69
00:03:47,589 --> 00:03:50,408
docker pull only downloads the image.

70
00:03:50,408 --> 00:03:54,750
The tag, one point thirty alpine, pins the exact version.

71
00:03:54,750 --> 00:03:56,044
Always use a tag.

72
00:03:56,600 --> 00:03:58,832
docker images lists what you have.

73
00:03:58,832 --> 00:04:00,737
In Docker 29 the columns are:

74
00:04:00,737 --> 00:04:03,823
the image name and tag, its ID, the disk usage,

75
00:04:03,823 --> 00:04:09,994
the downloaded content size, and an extra column that shows U when a container uses the image.

76
00:04:10,533 --> 00:04:17,001
Now docker run, with dash d for detached, so it runs in the background, and dash dash name web.

77
00:04:17,001 --> 00:04:21,699
Docker prints the new container's ID, and docker ps shows it running:

78
00:04:21,699 --> 00:04:27,826
the image, the command, when it was created, the status, the ports, and the name we chose.

79
00:04:29,567 --> 00:04:31,907
Two commands you will use every day.

80
00:04:31,907 --> 00:04:36,198
docker logs shows everything the container's main process printed.

81
00:04:36,198 --> 00:04:38,928
Here, nginx starting its worker processes.

82
00:04:39,467 --> 00:04:43,665
docker exec runs an extra command inside a running container.

83
00:04:43,665 --> 00:04:45,730
ps lists the processes inside:

84
00:04:45,730 --> 00:04:48,965
process number one is the nginx master process.

85
00:04:48,965 --> 00:04:53,233
Inside its own world, the container's main process is P I D 1.

86
00:04:53,767 --> 00:04:56,605
And the hostname of the container is its short ID.

87
00:04:56,605 --> 00:04:58,648
Remember that, we will use it later.

88
00:05:00,400 --> 00:05:03,078
Containers move through a small set of states.

89
00:05:03,078 --> 00:05:06,919
docker create makes a container from an image without starting it.

90
00:05:07,467 --> 00:05:09,246
Its state is Created.

91
00:05:09,800 --> 00:05:12,369
docker start runs its main process:

92
00:05:12,369 --> 00:05:12,957
Running.

93
00:05:12,957 --> 00:05:15,820
docker run is simply create plus start.

94
00:05:16,367 --> 00:05:21,002
When the main process ends, by itself or because you ran docker stop,

95
00:05:21,002 --> 00:05:23,891
the container is Exited, with an exit code.

96
00:05:23,891 --> 00:05:27,921
It still exists, with its files, and you can start it again.

97
00:05:28,467 --> 00:05:31,081
Only docker rm removes it for good.

98
00:05:32,833 --> 00:05:34,149
Let's break things.

99
00:05:34,149 --> 00:05:39,410
docker rm on a running container fails, and the error tells you exactly why:

100
00:05:39,410 --> 00:05:43,287
the container is running, stop it first or force remove.

101
00:05:43,287 --> 00:05:44,118
Read errors.

102
00:05:44,118 --> 00:05:46,333
They usually contain the answer.

103
00:05:46,867 --> 00:05:50,206
Now a container whose main process is sleep 5.

104
00:05:50,206 --> 00:05:53,473
Five seconds later, it is Exited with code 0.

105
00:05:53,473 --> 00:05:54,634
Nothing crashed:

106
00:05:54,634 --> 00:05:59,643
its only job was to sleep, and when the job ends, the container ends.

107
00:06:00,200 --> 00:06:02,812
This one surprises every beginner.

108
00:06:02,812 --> 00:06:07,037
docker run ubuntu, and the container exits immediately.

109
00:06:07,037 --> 00:06:07,344
Why?

110
00:06:07,900 --> 00:06:09,062
Inspect the image:

111
00:06:09,062 --> 00:06:10,870
its default command is bash.

112
00:06:10,870 --> 00:06:17,585
Without an interactive terminal, bash has nothing to read, so it exits, and the container exits with it.

113
00:06:17,585 --> 00:06:21,071
A container lives exactly as long as its main process.

114
00:06:21,071 --> 00:06:24,816
That one sentence explains half of all container problems.

115
00:06:26,567 --> 00:06:29,939
You can also work inside a container interactively.

116
00:06:29,939 --> 00:06:35,560
Dash i keeps input open, dash t gives you a terminal, and bash is the program to run.

117
00:06:35,560 --> 00:06:40,586
You get a prompt inside Ubuntu, even if your computer runs Windows or macOS.

118
00:06:41,133 --> 00:06:43,215
Type exit, and bash ends.

119
00:06:43,215 --> 00:06:47,712
bash was the main process, so the container is Exited.

120
00:06:47,712 --> 00:06:49,378
Same rule as before.

121
00:06:49,933 --> 00:06:52,698
And for a single command, no shell needed:

122
00:06:52,698 --> 00:06:55,989
docker run dash dash rm ubuntu cat etc os release.

123
00:06:55,989 --> 00:07:03,033
Dash dash rm deletes the container as soon as it stops, so you do not collect dozens of stopped containers.

124
00:07:04,767 --> 00:07:08,995
The web server is running, but can your browser reach it?

125
00:07:08,995 --> 00:07:09,589
Not yet.

126
00:07:09,589 --> 00:07:11,962
A container has its own network.

127
00:07:11,962 --> 00:07:14,336
To reach it, you publish a port.

128
00:07:14,900 --> 00:07:18,000
dash p eight zero eight zero colon eighty means:

129
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:22,844
traffic to port 8080 on your computer goes to port 80 inside the container.

130
00:07:23,400 --> 00:07:24,913
The order matters.

131
00:07:24,913 --> 00:07:27,349
Host first, container second.

132
00:07:27,349 --> 00:07:30,794
nginx listens on 80 inside its container.

133
00:07:31,333 --> 00:07:35,193
Without dash p, nothing outside the container can reach it.

134
00:07:36,933 --> 00:07:40,648
Without a published port, curl gets connection refused.

135
00:07:41,200 --> 00:07:46,224
With dash p eight zero eight one colon eighty, here is the nginx welcome page.

136
00:07:46,767 --> 00:07:48,752
Now we break it on purpose:

137
00:07:48,752 --> 00:07:52,871
dash p eight zero eight two colon eight zero eight zero.

138
00:07:52,871 --> 00:07:58,386
The container starts fine, docker ps says Up, but curl gets an empty reply.

139
00:07:58,386 --> 00:07:59,269
Don't guess.

140
00:07:59,269 --> 00:08:00,151
Investigate.

141
00:08:00,700 --> 00:08:03,978
Which port does the image actually use?

142
00:08:03,978 --> 00:08:05,659
docker inspect says:

143
00:08:05,659 --> 00:08:06,920
80 slash T C P.

144
00:08:06,920 --> 00:08:10,366
We mapped to 8080, where nothing listens.

145
00:08:10,366 --> 00:08:12,299
The container was fine.

146
00:08:12,299 --> 00:08:14,148
The mapping was wrong.

147
00:08:14,148 --> 00:08:16,669
Fix the mapping, and it works.

148
00:08:18,433 --> 00:08:20,731
And the same page in a browser:

149
00:08:20,731 --> 00:08:22,287
localhost, port 8080.

150
00:08:22,287 --> 00:08:28,142
Your computer forwards it to port 80 inside the container, where nginx answers.

151
00:08:28,142 --> 00:08:30,810
Your first containerised web server.

152
00:08:32,567 --> 00:08:33,896
Another classic.

153
00:08:33,896 --> 00:08:36,970
A second container on host port 8080.

154
00:08:36,970 --> 00:08:38,216
Docker refuses:

155
00:08:38,216 --> 00:08:44,779
Bind for 0 point 0 point 0 point 0 port 8080 failed, port is already allocated.

156
00:08:44,779 --> 00:08:48,351
One host port can only go to one container.

157
00:08:48,900 --> 00:08:49,932
Who has it?

158
00:08:49,932 --> 00:08:53,965
docker ps, filtered by published port 8080:

159
00:08:53,965 --> 00:08:56,310
our first container, web.

160
00:08:56,867 --> 00:08:57,598
And notice:

161
00:08:57,598 --> 00:09:01,054
the failed container still exists, in state Created.

162
00:09:01,054 --> 00:09:03,646
Remove it, then pick another host port.

163
00:09:03,646 --> 00:09:07,766
This is exactly the kind of problem you meet on a real server.

164
00:09:09,500 --> 00:09:11,934
Same image, different configuration.

165
00:09:11,934 --> 00:09:14,842
That is what environment variables are for.

166
00:09:14,842 --> 00:09:19,440
Here is our small Python app, simple app, running with its defaults:

167
00:09:19,440 --> 00:09:20,995
environment production.

168
00:09:20,995 --> 00:09:24,308
The hostname line shows which container answered.

169
00:09:24,867 --> 00:09:31,934
A second container from the exact same image, with dash e APP_ENV equals development and a different greeting.

170
00:09:31,934 --> 00:09:36,367
Now it greets with Good morning from Docker, environment development.

171
00:09:36,367 --> 00:09:37,074
No rebuild.

172
00:09:37,074 --> 00:09:40,222
The image did not change, only its configuration.

173
00:09:41,967 --> 00:09:45,122
When there are many settings, put them in a file,

174
00:09:45,122 --> 00:09:49,501
one name equals value per line, and pass it with dash dash env file.

175
00:09:49,501 --> 00:09:53,752
The env command inside the container shows both variables arrived,

176
00:09:53,752 --> 00:09:57,294
next to the ones Docker sets itself, like the hostname.

177
00:09:57,294 --> 00:10:00,514
Never commit env files with real passwords to Git.

178
00:10:00,514 --> 00:10:04,313
The repository's dot git ignore file already excludes them.

179
00:10:06,067 --> 00:10:10,795
Some images refuse to start without configuration, and that is a good thing.

180
00:10:10,795 --> 00:10:12,910
Run PostgreSQL without a password:

181
00:10:12,910 --> 00:10:17,639
it prints Database is uninitialized and superuser password is not specified,

182
00:10:17,639 --> 00:10:20,376
and tells you exactly which variable to set.

183
00:10:20,933 --> 00:10:24,005
docker ps dash a shows it Exited with code 1.

184
00:10:24,005 --> 00:10:28,168
When a container stops, the logs are the first place to look.

185
00:10:28,733 --> 00:10:31,658
With dash e POSTGRES_PASSWORD it starts.

186
00:10:31,658 --> 00:10:34,218
But look what docker inspect shows:

187
00:10:34,218 --> 00:10:39,118
the password, in plain text, for anyone who can run docker inspect.

188
00:10:39,118 --> 00:10:40,141
Remember this.

189
00:10:40,141 --> 00:10:43,798
We fix it properly in part two, with secret files.

190
00:10:45,533 --> 00:10:47,999
So far we used other people's images.

191
00:10:47,999 --> 00:10:55,331
Now we build our own, one instruction at a time, the way the tutorial does it in dockerfile steps one to four.

192
00:10:55,331 --> 00:10:58,130
Every Dockerfile starts FROM a base image:

193
00:10:58,130 --> 00:11:00,863
here the official slim Python 3.14 image.

194
00:11:01,400 --> 00:11:06,590
WORKDIR sets the folder inside the image, and COPY copies our code into it.

195
00:11:07,133 --> 00:11:10,688
RUN executes a command while the image is being built.

196
00:11:10,688 --> 00:11:14,243
Here it installs Flask, the one library the app needs.

197
00:11:14,800 --> 00:11:19,375
ENV sets a default configuration, and EXPOSE documents the port.

198
00:11:19,375 --> 00:11:23,235
EXPOSE alone publishes nothing, you still need dash p.

199
00:11:23,800 --> 00:11:26,999
And CMD is what runs when a container starts.

200
00:11:26,999 --> 00:11:30,055
RUN happens at build time, CMD at run time.

201
00:11:30,055 --> 00:11:34,250
Mixing them up is one of the most common beginner mistakes.

202
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:39,251
Step two of the tutorial has no RUN pip install.

203
00:11:39,251 --> 00:11:42,977
It builds without any error, but the container crashes:

204
00:11:42,977 --> 00:11:45,890
ModuleNotFoundError, no module named flask.

205
00:11:45,890 --> 00:11:49,683
It works on my laptop, because Flask is installed there.

206
00:11:49,683 --> 00:11:53,545
The image only contains what the Dockerfile puts into it.

207
00:11:54,100 --> 00:11:56,226
Step three adds the install.

208
00:11:56,226 --> 00:12:00,630
Build, run with dash p five thousand, and the app answers.

209
00:12:01,167 --> 00:12:03,189
One more pair you must know:

210
00:12:03,189 --> 00:12:07,596
ENTRYPOINT is the fixed program, CMD is its default argument.

211
00:12:07,596 --> 00:12:11,858
This little image has ENTRYPOINT echo Hello, and CMD world.

212
00:12:11,858 --> 00:12:16,770
Run it with the word Docker, and the argument replaces only the CMD:

213
00:12:16,770 --> 00:12:17,781
Hello, Docker.

214
00:12:19,533 --> 00:12:25,571
Every instruction in a Dockerfile creates a layer, and docker history shows them, newest on top.

215
00:12:25,571 --> 00:12:30,100
Our four instructions sit on top of the layers of the Python base image.

216
00:12:30,100 --> 00:12:32,993
Of our own layers, pip install is the big one.

217
00:12:33,533 --> 00:12:34,691
Layers are cached.

218
00:12:34,691 --> 00:12:39,321
When you rebuild, Docker reuses every layer whose inputs did not change.

219
00:12:39,321 --> 00:12:43,565
But as soon as one layer changes, every layer after it is rebuilt.

220
00:12:43,565 --> 00:12:47,810
So the order of the instructions decides how fast your builds are.

221
00:12:49,567 --> 00:12:50,594
Let's prove it.

222
00:12:50,594 --> 00:12:55,115
I changed one comment line in app.py, and rebuilt with step three,

223
00:12:55,115 --> 00:12:59,773
which copies all the code first and installs the libraries after it.

224
00:12:59,773 --> 00:13:04,636
The code changed, so the copy layer changed, so pip install runs again.

225
00:13:04,636 --> 00:13:08,198
Successfully installed Flask, for a one line change.

226
00:13:08,733 --> 00:13:13,276
Step five copies only the requirements file first, installs, and copies the code last.

227
00:13:13,276 --> 00:13:15,547
Same change, and now pip install is CACHED.

228
00:13:15,547 --> 00:13:17,502
Only the last, tiny layer is rebuilt.

229
00:13:17,502 --> 00:13:18,241
Rule of thumb:

230
00:13:18,241 --> 00:13:21,199
things that rarely change go first, your code goes last.

231
00:13:21,199 --> 00:13:26,165
In a real project, that is the difference between a five second build and a five minute build.

232
00:13:27,900 --> 00:13:31,414
The dot at the end of docker build is the build context:

233
00:13:31,414 --> 00:13:33,862
the folder Docker sends to the builder.

234
00:13:33,862 --> 00:13:37,125
Watch what happens without a dot docker ignore file,

235
00:13:37,125 --> 00:13:42,396
when a 50 megabyte junk folder and a .env file with a password sit next to the code.

236
00:13:42,396 --> 00:13:43,714
Transferring context:

237
00:13:43,714 --> 00:13:44,529
50 megabytes.

238
00:13:45,067 --> 00:13:50,327
And because the Dockerfile copies everything, the password is now inside the image.

239
00:13:50,327 --> 00:13:52,862
Anyone who pulls this image can read it.

240
00:13:52,862 --> 00:13:54,826
This happens in real companies.

241
00:13:55,367 --> 00:13:58,881
Put the dot docker ignore file back, and rebuild:

242
00:13:58,881 --> 00:14:03,973
63 bytes of context, and the image contains only the application files.

243
00:14:03,973 --> 00:14:08,706
Faster builds, smaller images, and no secrets shipped by accident.

244
00:14:10,467 --> 00:14:13,466
Where would you use this as a DevOps engineer?

245
00:14:13,466 --> 00:14:14,183
Everywhere.

246
00:14:14,183 --> 00:14:17,313
The image is the artifact every pipeline builds,

247
00:14:17,313 --> 00:14:21,486
scans and ships, and the Dockerfile makes that build repeatable.

248
00:14:22,033 --> 00:14:27,251
Ports and environment variables let the same image run in development, test and production.

249
00:14:27,251 --> 00:14:32,469
And when something breaks, docker logs and docker exec are the first two commands you type.

250
00:14:33,033 --> 00:14:36,050
Layer order decides how long every pipeline run takes,

251
00:14:36,050 --> 00:14:40,463
and a dot docker ignore file keeps keys and junk out of the images you publish.

252
00:14:42,200 --> 00:14:43,547
Let's recap part one.

253
00:14:43,547 --> 00:14:46,691
You can pull and run containers, read their logs,

254
00:14:46,691 --> 00:14:51,825
look inside them, and you know why a container stops when its main process ends.

255
00:14:52,367 --> 00:14:56,680
You can publish ports, configure containers with environment variables,

256
00:14:56,680 --> 00:15:00,263
and you can write a Dockerfile, instruction by instruction.

257
00:15:00,800 --> 00:15:03,163
You know how layers and the build cache work,

258
00:15:03,163 --> 00:15:07,994
and how a dot docker ignore file keeps your builds fast and your secrets out of your images.

259
00:15:08,533 --> 00:15:09,360
In part two:

260
00:15:09,360 --> 00:15:12,461
data that survives, networks, Docker Compose,

261
00:15:12,461 --> 00:15:19,973
security, image optimization, real troubleshooting, and the capstone, a complete three container application.

262
00:15:19,973 --> 00:15:24,107
Before you continue, do labs one to eight in the repository.

263
00:15:24,107 --> 00:15:28,173
Typing the commands yourself is where the learning happens.

264
00:15:28,173 --> 00:15:29,551
See you in part two.
